{"type": "say", "text": "Good morning, everyone. Thank you for being here. As you know, a former sales executive departed last week and joined a direct competitor, and we have reason to believe confidential customer information may have been used after the departure. We may need to request a preliminary-injunction hearing within three weeks. I've called this case conference because, under our pre-filing practice, I need to hear from employment, forensics, and insurance counsel on any material issues before I can authorize the filing. The draft currently seeks broad relief covering customer information, pricing materials, and source-code documentation. I'd like to go around the room — let's start with employment counsel. Are there any material issues I should be aware of before we proceed?"}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF2", "text": "Elena, I need to flag a material issue from my forensic review this week. I reviewed the access logs and forensic image of the executive's activity, and I confirmed that the only company data copied to the executive's personal device was the customer-list database. My review did not show any copying of pricing materials or source-code documentation. I also want to note that I have not yet updated the evidence chart to reflect these findings. This is significant because the draft filing currently seeks broad relief covering customer information, pricing materials, and source-code documentation — but the forensic evidence only supports relief as to the customer-list database."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF3", "text": "Elena, I need to flag a material insurance issue. I confirmed with our broker this week that the applicable cyber-and-crime endorsement requires the carrier's written consent before the company seeks injunctive relief against an employee. This confirmation is still outside the litigation file, so it hasn't been documented in our matter records yet. We need to obtain the carrier's written consent before filing any injunction request, or we risk losing coverage for this matter."}
```

